Return prose, not JSON. Begin with exactly four short decision lines: DISPOSITION: ACCEPT|REVISE|REFUSE; PUBLISHABLE: YES|NO; FAILURE_LAYER: sources|assignment|instructions|deterministic-gates|writer-model|draft-repair|none; REPLAY_FROM: <layer>. Then write sections headed SUMMARY:, FINDINGS:, CLAIM AUDIT:, REQUIRED REPAIRS:, PIPELINE EVIDENCE:, and PIPELINE IMPROVEMENT:. End with END REPORT:. The four decision lines are machine-picked; all substantive judgment stays prose.

ACCEPT only when every material factual claim is supported by the supplied evidence, sources are visibly attributed or cited, deterministic errors are absent, the headline and lede are faithful, and the prose is specific, useful, non-repetitive, non-template, and not padded with confident abstractions.

REVISE when a bounded repair could make the piece publishable. REFUSE when its thesis or evidence basis is unsalvageable. Treat deterministic standards as necessary but not sufficient.

For a failed piece, locate the earliest causal layer that should change before replay: sources were too thin or incoherent; assignment/search lens was unsupported or distorting; instructions induced the defect; deterministic gates passed or failed the wrong property; the writer model lacked capability despite an adequate packet; or only a bounded draft repair remains. Do not blame the model for defects compelled by its packet. Do not blame sources when the pipeline discarded their available detail.

DETERMINISTIC STANDARDS is the authoritative reading of the current manuscript. PRODUCTION RUN is historical provenance. Never report a historical gate failure as a current defect when the current standards reading has retired or cleared that gate.

Pay special attention to invented causal claims, flattening distinct Indigenous peoples or regions into one analogy, attribution laundering, generic collapse rhetoric, SEO phrases forced into grammar, and conclusions that outrun the sources.

